rvfimon.v: fix clz/ctz reporting the wrong end of the word

hvl/common/rvfimon.v is generated by third_party/riscv-formal's
monitor/generate.py.  The generated clz64/clz32/ctz64/ctz32 helpers assign on
*every* set bit rather than stopping at the first, so the value left behind is
whichever bit the loop visited last -- the opposite end of the word from the
one the instruction is defined to report.

The Zbb instructions are only reachable once something actually executes them.
Booting Linux does: the boot died at 53.5M cycles on `ctz x6, x6` with
rs1 = 0xffffff0000000000, where the core returned 40 (correct) and the monitor
demanded 63.  The core was right and the shadow model was wrong.

Reversing the iteration order makes the final assignment the correct one, which
is a smaller change than adding an early exit and keeps the generated structure
recognisable.

This is applied by `make -C sim generated-files` and verified by
`make -C sim check-generated`, so regenerating the monitor does not silently
drop it.  Remove this patch once the fix is upstream in riscv-formal and the
submodule has been bumped past it.

--- a/hvl/common/rvfimon.v
+++ b/hvl/common/rvfimon.v
@@ -14223,7 +14223,12 @@
     integer i;
     begin
       clz64 = 64;
-      for (i = 63; i >= 0; i = i - 1)
+      // AMOEBA local fix -- see testcode/linux/README.md.
+      // The generated loop assigns on *every* set bit instead of stopping at
+      // the first, so it ends up reporting the bit at the far end of the word:
+      // ctz returned the highest set bit and clz the lowest.  Reversing the
+      // iteration order makes the final assignment the correct one.
+      for (i = 0; i < 64; i = i + 1)
         if (v[i]) clz64 = 63 - i;
     end
   endfunction
@@ -14283,7 +14288,12 @@
     integer i;
     begin
       clz32 = 32;
-      for (i = 31; i >= 0; i = i - 1)
+      // AMOEBA local fix -- see testcode/linux/README.md.
+      // The generated loop assigns on *every* set bit instead of stopping at
+      // the first, so it ends up reporting the bit at the far end of the word:
+      // ctz returned the highest set bit and clz the lowest.  Reversing the
+      // iteration order makes the final assignment the correct one.
+      for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1)
         if (v[i]) clz32 = 31 - i;
     end
   endfunction
@@ -14467,7 +14477,12 @@
     integer i;
     begin
       ctz64 = 64;
-      for (i = 0; i < 64; i = i + 1)
+      // AMOEBA local fix -- see testcode/linux/README.md.
+      // The generated loop assigns on *every* set bit instead of stopping at
+      // the first, so it ends up reporting the bit at the far end of the word:
+      // ctz returned the highest set bit and clz the lowest.  Reversing the
+      // iteration order makes the final assignment the correct one.
+      for (i = 63; i >= 0; i = i - 1)
         if (v[i]) ctz64 = i;
     end
   endfunction
@@ -14527,7 +14542,12 @@
     integer i;
     begin
       ctz32 = 32;
-      for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1)
+      // AMOEBA local fix -- see testcode/linux/README.md.
+      // The generated loop assigns on *every* set bit instead of stopping at
+      // the first, so it ends up reporting the bit at the far end of the word:
+      // ctz returned the highest set bit and clz the lowest.  Reversing the
+      // iteration order makes the final assignment the correct one.
+      for (i = 31; i >= 0; i = i - 1)
         if (v[i]) ctz32 = i;
     end
   endfunction
